  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will dominate the region today, forcing the return\nof dry conditions and seasonal temperatures. Saturday, too, now\nlooks to be drier, though there remain low chances for light \nshowers in the Mid-Hudson Valley and western New England due to \na shortwave passing by to our south and a surface low tracking \nslowly north and east along the New England Coastline. Based on\nthe drier trend in the latest guidance, we capped PoPs at slight\nchance for these areas. \n\nThe latter half of the weekend into the beginning of next week\nwill not be as pleasant. A deepening, negatively-tilting trough\nwill begin to dig into the eastern CONUS on Saturday as an\nassociated surface low develops in the Great Lakes. A continuous\ndeepening of the upper trough will lead to its closing off while\nthe cyclone at the surface simultaneously deepens and tracks \nnortheastward into southwest Quebec through Sunday afternoon. \nMeanwhile, a secondary surface low looks to develop off the Mid-\nAtlantic/Long Island Coasts early Sunday morning, weakening into\nan open wave pulsing about the southern periphery of the primary by\nSunday afternoon. Both the enhanced low-level convergence and \nupper- level forcing from these features will be sufficient to \ngenerate fairly widespread showers across the region by Sunday \nmorning. These showers, and potentially a few embedded\nthunderstorms, will continue intermittently throughout the day\nbefore drier air cycles through the low aloft and upslope flow\nmaintains light rain solely across higher terrain areas.\nMoist, cyclonic flow will continue through the beginning of the\nweek as the upper low remains fairly stationary overhead.\nIntermittent showers will therefore continue through Tuesday,\nthough the greatest concentration of these will be across higher\nterrain areas. The system finally departs to our northeast by \nWednesday morning, forcing the return of drier conditions. \n\nDue to the northern-stream nature of this system, there's a\ngeneral lack of moisture content to drive significant rainfall.\nOf course, if a thunderstorm or two should form on Sunday, there\ncould be some heavier downpours, but overall precipitation rates\nwith any showers Sunday through Tuesday should be rather light. \nAdditionally, due to the cycling of dry air through the system, \nnone of these days are expected to be washouts. Therefore, any\nand all rain will be beneficial and will not drive any flooding\nconcerns.",
